e_demo/publication/context_sources_da7c52f85774421b234f5ca0.json.gz","sha256":"7934807adf42137871f47f93e3ed8370250a8877c8781aec9f718a2e49d704c4"},"text":"Central Intelligence Bulletins were Central Intelligence Agency summaries of international developments. This collection spans 28 February 1951 to 13 June 1961. Paired declassified releases let readers compare passages visible earlier with material revealed later. The reports record what intelligence analysts were saying at the time, including uncertainty and disagreement about people, governments and events."},"iran":{"sources_ref":{"path":"experiments/narrative_demo/publication/context_sources_da7c52f85774421b234f5ca0.json.gz","sha256":"7934807adf42137871f47f93e3ed8370250a8877c8781aec9f718a2e49d704c4"},"text":"These diplomatic cables follow Iran's political and economic crisis through reports dated 8 June 1977 to 22 November 1979. They describe government institutions, opposition figures, military leaders and problems affecting everyday operations. Individual reports offer particular observations and perspectives. The demonstration's missing cable passages are synthetic exercises, with the original wording retained for comparison."},"vietnam":{"sources_ref":{"path":"experiments/narrative_demo/publication/context_sources_da7c52f85774421b234f5ca0.json.gz","sha256":"7934807adf42137871f47f93e3ed8370250a8877c8781aec9f718a2e49d704c4"},"text":"This diplomatic cable collection contains reports dated 9 January 1973 to 26 July 1978 about Vietnam and related regional developments. The selected investigation concerns South Vietnam's government, economy, military and political opposition in the 1970s. Its missing passages are synthetic exercises. This is a separate collection from the earlier South Vietnam episode in the intelligence bulletins."}},"narratives":{"cib_burma_extra":{"no_new_tour_asserted":true,"presentation_only":true,"provenance_mode":"QUERY_ONLY_SYNOPSIS_NO_ADDITIONAL_HISTORICAL_ASSERTIONS","source_proof_acceptance_ref":{"path":"experiments/narrative_parallel/independent_review/takeover_20261119/aung_extra_source_preparation_review.json","sha256":"7d2ee71c39b4b2d9e25edd17fbd51a9533b67ea3691fecc75a9442edfd3e953e"},"sources_ref":{"path":"experiments/narrative_demo/publication/context_sources_da7c52f85774421b234f5ca0.json.gz","sha256":"7934807adf42137871f47f93e3ed8370250a8877c8781aec9f718a2e49d704c4"},"text":"The bulletin describes alleged military cooperation between Burma and China while preserving an official denial. It raises questions about how the agreement was arranged and what senior officials knew about it."},"cib_salvador_bench":{"sources_ref":{"path":"experiments/narrative_demo/publication/context_sources_da7c52f85774421b234f5ca0.json.gz","sha256":"7934807adf42137871f47f93e3ed8370250a8877c8781aec9f718a2e49d704c4"},"text":"El Salvador's provisional government emerged from the ouster of President Lemus in October 1960 and combined military and civilian members. Subsequent accounts describe competing political groups seeking influence over the new order, with labor organizations, students, and military figures taking different positions. Questions about rural political activity and links with Cuba sit beside arguments over government appointments and the institutions responsible for internal security. Reports sometimes describe an intention or a claim, rather than a completed action, and assessments of political influence do not necessarily agree. This investigation examines how those different kinds of statement fit together without assuming that every group shared one plan or that every reported journey was completed. Reading separate accounts can clarify the order of developments and the scope of particular claims. It can also preserve disagreement and uncertainty that disappear when a complicated political episode is reduced to a single explanation."}},"presentation_only":true},"presentation_context_sources_ref":{"path":"experiments/narrative_demo/publication/context_sources_da7c52f85774421b234f5ca0.json.gz","sha256":"7934807adf42137871f47f93e3ed8370250a8877c8781aec9f718a2e49d704c4"},"presentation_tours":{"cib_salvador_bench":{"anchor_searches":["El Salvador","PRAM","Mario Castrillo Zeledon"],"approved_spoiler_safe":true,"collection":"bench","corpus":"cib","cutoff_exclusive":"1961-01-17","duration_seconds":75,"map_transport_and_live_clicks_verified":false,"narrative":"Salvadoran political vulnerability","native_index":{"path":"experiments/current_refresh/resource_handoff/indexes/current_index_0c8587c8fa8e78ddad1c24cc/graph.sqlite3","sha256":"b152ab5f086cfa120c5d4ece5781f6e7d5bf09827b8de0daa6311c594e66b980"},"presentation_only_never_inference_input":true,"release_clearance":false,"resource_id":"cib_resource_623c60e1e128944377f0","sequence":["Select CIB, earlier-access view and 17 January 1961 exclusive cutoff.","Search El Salvador; inspect country provenance and center on all direct connections.","Use a clearly labelled new search for Preparation of countercoup. Inspect its source-backed officers edge and the November quotation.","Use a clearly labelled new search for the military-dissension claim. Inspect its own edge and December quotation; do not claim a path between the two searches.","Show date/access filters. Hierarchy is retrospective navigation, not contemporaneous evidence.","Open the Salvador bench workbench: compare the announced rural organizing plan with the reported journey, then test the reported appointment, then inspect the concealed description."],"source_tour_reference":{"path":"experiments/narrative_parallel/cib_anchors/spoiler_free_tour.json","sha256":"9ddf326459e56fce6949de00fec22e547541c94d47f41bd08de0c37d07084e29"},"stops":[{"actual_edge_id":"semantic_edge_4af3f27090273d3b76af:relation_evidence_bc29a981d686d137223a","cross_stop_traversal_claimed":false,"nodes":[{"hierarchy":{"community_id":"community_d80d8eeed7de4da41c94","domain_id":"domain_cff029d44c164d1553fb","theme_id":"theme_8f579cc248725a4fcfa